What is C++ "enterprise architecture"?

**C++ enterprise architecture** isn't a specific C++ standard or framework. 
It means the **architecture and engineering practices used to build large, long-lived C++ systems** in organizations.

Think of it as:

> **How do we structure a large C++ application so many developers can work on it, 
maintain it, test it, deploy it, and change it for years?**

### Typical structure

For a non-embedded backend application, you might see:


                    REST / CLI / GUI
                          │
                          ▼
                  ┌───────────────┐
                  │  Controller   │
                  └───────┬───────┘
                          │
                          ▼
                  ┌───────────────┐
                  │    Service    │
                  └───────┬───────┘
                          │
             ┌────────────┴────────────┐
             ▼                         ▼
      ┌──────────────┐         ┌──────────────┐
      │   Domain     │         │  Repository  │
      │    Logic     │         │              │
      └──────────────┘         └──────┬───────┘
                                      │
                             ┌────────┴────────┐
                             ▼                 ▼
                          PostgreSQL         Redis


The important idea is that **each layer has a responsibility**.

### Example

Instead of putting everything into `main.cpp`:

int main()
{
    // HTTP request
    // validation
    // business logic
    // database
    // logging
    // response
}

an enterprise-style application might have:

main.cpp
   ↓
UserController
   ↓
UserService
   ↓
UserRepository
   ↓
PostgreSQL

And the actual business logic could remain ordinary C++:

class UserService
{
public:
    User createUser(const UserData& data)
    {
        validate(data);

        User user(data);
        repository.save(user);

        return user;
    }

private:
    UserRepository repository;
};

### Why so much abstraction?

Large companies have concerns that a small application doesn't:

| Concern             | Why it matters                          |
| ------------------- | --------------------------------------- |
| Multiple developers | Separate responsibilities               |
| Testing             | Replace dependencies with test doubles  |
| Database changes    | Avoid database code everywhere          |
| API changes         | Keep business logic independent         |
| Multiple interfaces | REST, CLI, GUI can share core logic     |
| Logging             | Centralized observability               |
| Security            | Authentication/authorization boundaries |
| Deployment          | Different environments/configurations   |
| Long lifetime       | Code may be maintained for years        |

That's where interfaces, dependency injection, repositories, factories, modules, etc. can become useful.

### But this is the important part

**Enterprise architecture ≠ more classes.**

Bad:

Controller
    ↓
Service
    ↓
ServiceManager
    ↓
ServiceProvider
    ↓
ServiceFactory
    ↓
RepositoryManager
    ↓
Repository

when all of that ultimately does:

return database.getUser(id);

Good architecture is closer to:

API
 │
 ▼
Service
 │
 ▼
Domain
 │
 ▼
Repository
 │
 ▼
Database

with abstractions introduced **where they provide a real benefit**.

For the kind of C++ architecture — 
**one core application that can later have Console UI, GUI, or REST API** — 
you don't need to immediately build a huge "enterprise architecture."

A very reasonable starting point is:

             Console UI
                 │
GUI ─────────────┼───────────── REST API
                 │
                 ▼
          Application/Core
                 │
          ┌──────┴──────┐
          ▼             ▼
       Domain       Infrastructure
                        │
                  DB / Redis / HTTP

That gives you **separation without unnecessary abstraction**.
